/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/rhcsa.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.register({"id":"rhcsa","vendor":"Red Hat","name":"Red Hat Certified System Administrator","short":"RHCSA","exam":"EX200 (RHEL 10)","blurb":"Hands-on Linux administration exam on Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10: shell tools, software, storage, boot, networking, users and SELinux, for new Linux and cloud system administrators.","status":"check","statusNote":"redhat.com was blocked by the network proxy during this check on Sept 24, 2026, so the EX200 page could not be read directly. Search results quoting Red Hat's EX200 page say the exam is now based on RHEL 10, lists ten objective groups, adds Flatpak repositories/packages and systemd timer scheduling, and no longer includes the containers (Podman) group. Red Hat publishes no weights for this performance-based exam: the weights here are estimates based on how many objectives each group has and how often the group shows up in hands-on tasks. Exam length (3 hours) and the 210/300 pass mark also come from search results. Confirm the objective list, duration and score on the EX200 page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"Performance-based: about 15–20 hands-on tasks on live RHEL 10 systems (count not published)","minutes":180,"pass":"210 of 300 points (70%)","extra":"The real EX200 has no multiple-choice questions. You configure real RHEL 10 machines, and everything must still work after a reboot. The practice exam on this site is a 60-question, 90-minute knowledge check of the same objectives (commands, config files, SELinux and troubleshooting). Pair it with timed hands-on practice on a free RHEL 10 VM from the Red Hat Developer subscription, or on AlmaLinux or Rocky Linux 10."},"examSim":{"questions":60,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"Red Hat EX200 exam page (objectives)","url":"https://www.redhat.com/en/services/training/ex200-red-hat-certified-system-administrator-rhcsa-exam"},{"label":"Red Hat RHCSA certification page","url":"https://www.redhat.com/en/services/certification/rhcsa"},{"label":"Free RHEL for individuals (Red Hat Developer)","url":"https://developers.redhat.com/products/rhel/download"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"8–10","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Understand and use essential tools","w":12,"topics":["Using a shell prompt and running commands with correct syntax","Input and output redirection: >, >>, 2>, 2>&1, <, pipes and tee","grep and regular expressions: ^, $, ., *, [ ], -i, -v, -r, -E","Accessing remote systems with ssh; logging in and switching users (su -, sudo -i) in multiuser targets","Archiving and compressing with tar, gzip, bzip2 and xz (-c, -x, -t, -z, -j, -J, -f)","Creating and editing text files with vim or nano","Creating, deleting, copying and moving files and directories (mkdir -p, cp -a, mv, rm -r)","Hard links vs symbolic links (ln, ln -s) and their limits","Listing, setting and changing standard ugo/rwx permissions in numeric and symbolic form","Finding documentation with man, man -k, info and /usr/share/doc"],"notes":["Objective group: Understand and use essential tools"],"labs":["On a free RHEL 10 Developer VM (or AlmaLinux/Rocky 10), use `grep -E` on /etc/passwd to list users whose shell is /bin/bash, and send the result plus any errors to one file with `> out.txt 2>&1`.","Archive /etc into /root/etc.tar.xz with tar and xz, list its contents without extracting, then extract only /etc/hosts into /tmp/restore.","Create a file, a hard link and a symlink to it, delete the original and explain with `ls -li` why one link still works and the other is broken."]},{"id":2,"name":"Manage software","w":7,"topics":["Configuring access to RPM repositories: .repo files in /etc/yum.repos.d/ (baseurl, enabled, gpgcheck, gpgkey)","dnf config-manager --add-repo, dnf repolist and dnf clean all","Installing, updating and removing RPM packages with dnf (install, remove, update, reinstall, history undo)","Finding packages and files: dnf search, dnf provides, dnf info","Querying installed packages with rpm -q, -qa, -qi, -ql, -qf, -qc","Package groups: dnf group list and dnf group install","Configuring access to Flatpak repositories: flatpak remote-add, flatpak remotes","Installing, listing, updating and removing Flatpak applications (flatpak install, list, update, uninstall, run)"],"notes":["Objective group: Manage software (RPM and Flatpak)"],"labs":["Loop-mount a RHEL 10 or AlmaLinux 10 DVD ISO, write a .repo file for its BaseOS and AppStream folders, disable other repos and install a package from it.","Use `dnf provides` to find which package supplies the `semanage` command, install it, then confirm with `rpm -qf $(which semanage)`.","Add the Flathub remote with `flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists`, install a small app, list it, and uninstall it."]},{"id":3,"name":"Create simple shell scripts","w":7,"topics":["Shebang lines, making scripts executable and running them from PATH","Conditionally running code with if, elif, else and test / [ ] (-f, -d, -z, -eq, -gt, string compares)","Exit status ($?), && and ||, and exit codes in scripts","Looping with for (over lists, globs, $(seq)) and while read","Processing script inputs: $1, $2, $#, $@ and $0","Processing the output of shell commands with $( ) command substitution","Reading input and using variables and quoting correctly","case statements for simple argument handling"],"notes":["Objective group: Create simple shell scripts"],"labs":["Write /usr/local/bin/userinfo.sh that prints usage and exits 1 when no argument is given, otherwise prints the UID and shell of the user named in $1.","Write a for loop that creates user1–user5 with `useradd` and sets each password from a variable, then a second loop that deletes them.","Write a script that reads a list of hostnames from a file with `while read` and reports which ones answer a single ping."]},{"id":4,"name":"Operate running systems","w":12,"topics":["Booting, rebooting and shutting down normally (systemctl reboot, poweroff)","Booting into different targets manually from the GRUB menu (systemd.unit=rescue.target, emergency.target)","Interrupting the boot process to gain access (rd.break, chroot /sysroot, passwd, touch /.autorelabel)","Identifying CPU- and memory-intensive processes with top, ps aux --sort and killing them (kill, pkill, signals 15 and 9)","Adjusting process scheduling with nice and renice (range -20 to 19)","Managing tuning profiles with tuned-adm (active, recommend, profile)","Locating and reading system logs: /var/log/messages, /var/log/secure, journalctl -u, -p, -b, --since","Preserving the systemd journal across reboots (Storage=persistent, /var/log/journal)","Starting, stopping and checking network services (systemctl status, ss -tlnp)","Securely transferring files between systems with scp, sftp and rsync over ssh"],"notes":["Objective group: Operate running systems"],"labs":["Reset a forgotten root password on your RHEL 10 or AlmaLinux VM using rd.break from the GRUB menu, and make sure SELinux relabels on the next boot.","Start a `sha1sum /dev/zero` CPU hog, find it with top, lower its priority with renice, then end it with kill.","Make the journal persistent, reboot, and show errors from the previous boot with `journalctl -b -1 -p err`."]},{"id":5,"name":"Configure local storage","w":12,"topics":["Listing disks and partitions with lsblk, blkid and fdisk -l","Creating and deleting GPT partitions with parted, gdisk or fdisk; setting the partition type (lvm, swap)","Creating and removing physical volumes (pvcreate, pvremove, pvs)","Creating volume groups and assigning physical volumes (vgcreate -s, vgextend, vgs)","Creating and deleting logical volumes by size (-L) or extent count (-l) (lvcreate, lvremove, lvs)","Mounting file systems at boot by UUID or label in /etc/fstab","Adding new partitions, logical volumes and swap without destroying existing data","Creating and enabling swap (mkswap, swapon, swapon --show) and making it persistent"],"notes":["Objective group: Configure local storage"],"labs":["Add a second virtual disk, create a GPT label and two partitions with parted, and make one an LVM physical volume.","Create volume group vgdata with 8 MiB extents, then a logical volume of exactly 60 extents, and format it with XFS.","Add a 512 MiB swap partition, activate it and add it to /etc/fstab by UUID, then prove it survives a reboot with `swapon --show`."]},{"id":6,"name":"Create and configure file systems","w":10,"topics":["Creating vfat, ext4 and xfs file systems (mkfs.vfat, mkfs.ext4, mkfs.xfs)","Mounting, unmounting and using file systems; mount -a and findmnt --verify","/etc/fstab fields: device, mount point, type, options, dump, fsck order","Mounting and unmounting NFS network file systems (mount -t nfs, _netdev)","Configuring autofs: /etc/auto.master.d/*.autofs, map files and wildcard (* and &) entries","Extending existing logical volumes and their file systems (lvextend -r, xfs_growfs, resize2fs)","XFS can grow but not shrink; ext4 can do both when unmounted","Diagnosing and correcting file permission problems (ls -l, namei -l, chmod, chown)"],"notes":["Objective group: Create and configure file systems"],"labs":["Build a small NFS server on a second AlmaLinux/Rocky VM, then mount an export on the client by hand and permanently with _netdev.","Configure autofs so that /shares/<name> mounts server:/exports/<name> on demand using a wildcard map entry.","Grow a mounted XFS logical volume by 300 MiB with `lvextend -r` and confirm with `df -h` that the file system grew too."]},{"id":7,"name":"Deploy, configure and maintain systems","w":10,"topics":["Scheduling tasks with at, cron (crontab -e, /etc/cron.d) and systemd timer units (OnCalendar=, OnBootSec=)","Starting and stopping services and configuring them to start at boot (systemctl enable --now, disable, mask)","Setting the default boot target (systemctl get-default, set-default)","Configuring time service clients with chronyd (/etc/chrony.conf, chronyc sources) and timedatectl","Installing and updating packages from the Red Hat CDN, a remote repository or the local file system","Registering systems with subscription-manager (Developer subscription)","Modifying the boot loader: grubby, /etc/default/grub and grub2-mkconfig","Choosing the default kernel and adding or removing kernel arguments"],"notes":["Objective group: Deploy, configure, and maintain systems"],"labs":["Write a systemd service and a .timer unit that runs a cleanup script every day at 02:30, enable the timer and check it with `systemctl list-timers`.","Point chronyd at a specific NTP server in /etc/chrony.conf, restart it and confirm synchronization with `chronyc sources -v`.","Use grubby to add a kernel argument to all kernels, reboot, check /proc/cmdline, then remove the argument."]},{"id":8,"name":"Manage basic networking","w":8,"topics":["Configuring static and DHCP IPv4 and IPv6 addresses with nmcli (ipv4.method manual/auto, ipv4.addresses, ipv4.gateway)","NetworkManager keyfiles in /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/ (RHEL 10 no longer uses ifcfg files)","Bringing connections up and down and making them autoconnect (nmcli con up, connection.autoconnect)","Configuring hostname resolution: hostnamectl, /etc/hosts, ipv4.dns and /etc/resolv.conf","Checking addresses, routes and name resolution: ip addr, ip route, ping, getent hosts","Configuring network services to start automatically at boot","Restricting network access with firewalld and firewall-cmd (services, ports, zones, --permanent, --reload)","Using nmtui as a text-based alternative to nmcli"],"notes":["Objective group: Manage basic networking"],"labs":["On a RHEL 10 or Rocky 10 VM, switch a connection from DHCP to a static IPv4 address, gateway and DNS with nmcli, and add an IPv6 address.","Set the hostname with hostnamectl, add a second host to /etc/hosts and resolve it with `getent hosts`.","Install httpd, open only the http service in the public zone permanently, and prove with `firewall-cmd --list-all` and curl from another VM."]},{"id":9,"name":"Manage users and groups","w":8,"topics":["Creating, deleting and modifying local user accounts (useradd -u -G -s -c, usermod, userdel -r)","Non-interactive shells for service accounts (/sbin/nologin)","Changing passwords (passwd, passwd --stdin) and adjusting password aging (chage -M -m -W -E -d 0)","Default aging in /etc/login.defs and account defaults in /etc/default/useradd and /etc/skel","Creating, deleting and modifying local groups and memberships (groupadd -g, usermod -aG, gpasswd)","Account databases: /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group; id and getent","Configuring superuser access: the wheel group, /etc/sudoers.d/ drop-ins and visudo","Locking and unlocking accounts (usermod -L, passwd -l, chage -E 0)"],"notes":["Objective group: Manage users and groups"],"labs":["Create group sysadmins (GID 3000), users alice and bob as members, and a user svc with no login shell; check with `id` and `getent group`.","Force alice to change her password at next login and set a 90-day maximum password age, then read it back with `chage -l`.","Give the sysadmins group full sudo rights through a file in /etc/sudoers.d/, check its syntax with `visudo -cf`, and test it."]},{"id":10,"name":"Manage security","w":14,"topics":["Configuring firewall settings with firewall-cmd: zones, services, ports, sources, runtime vs permanent","Managing default file permissions with umask (shell and /etc/login.defs, ~/.bashrc)","Configuring key-based SSH authentication (ssh-keygen, ssh-copy-id, ~/.ssh permissions, sshd_config)","Setting SELinux enforcing and permissive modes (getenforce, setenforce, /etc/selinux/config)","Listing and identifying SELinux file and process contexts (ls -Z, ps -eZ, id -Z)","Restoring default file contexts (restorecon -Rv) and adding rules with semanage fcontext","Managing SELinux port labels (semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp)","Using SELinux booleans (getsebool -a, setsebool -P, semanage boolean -l)","Diagnosing routine SELinux denials: /var/log/audit/audit.log, ausearch -m AVC, sealert"],"notes":["Objective group: Manage security (firewall, SSH, SELinux, umask)"],"labs":["Serve a web site from /web on your RHEL 10 or AlmaLinux VM: add an httpd_sys_content_t fcontext rule, run restorecon and confirm the page loads with SELinux enforcing.","Move httpd to TCP port 8088: label the port with semanage, update Listen, open the port in firewalld and find the AVC denial you get if you skip the label.","Set up key-based SSH from one VM to another, then set PasswordAuthentication no and confirm password logins are refused."]}],"study":{"1":[["What is the difference between `> file 2>&1` and `2>&1 > file`?","Redirections are processed left to right. `> file 2>&1` points stdout at the file, then points stderr where stdout now goes, so both land in the file. `2>&1 > file` copies stderr to the terminal (where stdout was) before stdout moves, so only stdout goes to the file."],["Compare hard links and symbolic links.","A hard link is another directory entry for the same inode: it keeps working if the original name is deleted, but it cannot cross file systems or point at a directory. A symlink is a small file holding a path: it can cross file systems and point to directories, but breaks if the target is removed."],["How do you create, list and extract a bzip2-compressed tar archive?","Create: `tar -cjf backup.tar.bz2 /dir`. List: `tar -tjf backup.tar.bz2` (or `tar -tf`, which detects compression). Extract: `tar -xjf backup.tar.bz2 -C /target`. Use -z for gzip and -J for xz."],["What does `chmod 2770 /shared` do compared with `chmod u=rwx,g=rwx,o= /shared`?","Both give rwx to user and group and nothing to others. The leading 2 in 2770 also sets the set-GID bit, so new files inherit the directory's group."],["How do you find a man page when you don't know the command name?","Use `man -k keyword` (same as `apropos`) to search page descriptions; run `mandb` if the index is empty. Section 5 covers file formats (`man 5 fstab`), section 8 admin commands. Extra docs live in /usr/share/doc."]],"2":[["Write a minimal .repo file for a local BaseOS mirror.","In /etc/yum.repos.d/local.repo:\n[BaseOS]\nname=Local BaseOS\nbaseurl=file:///mnt/iso/BaseOS\nenabled=1\ngpgcheck=0\nThen check with `dnf repolist`. For a web repo use an http(s) baseurl, and set gpgcheck=1 with gpgkey= in production."],["How do you find which package provides a file or command that isn't installed yet?","`dnf provides '*/semanage'` (or `dnf provides /usr/sbin/semanage`). For a file already on disk, `rpm -qf /path/to/file` names the installed package that owns it."],["What are the main Flatpak commands you need for the exam?","Add a remote: `flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists name URL`. List remotes: `flatpak remotes`. Install: `flatpak install remote app-id`. List: `flatpak list`. Update: `flatpak update`. Remove: `flatpak uninstall app-id`. Run: `flatpak run app-id`."],["How do you undo a package transaction that broke something?","Run `dnf history` to find the transaction ID, then `dnf history undo <ID>` to reverse it. `dnf history info <ID>` shows what it changed."]],"3":[["What do $0, $1, $#, $@ and $? hold in a script?","$0 is the script name, $1 the first argument, $# the number of arguments, $@ all arguments as separate words, and $? the exit status of the last command (0 means success)."],["Write an if statement that exits with status 1 when no argument is given.","if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo \"Usage: $0 name\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n(Or `[ -z \"$1\" ]`.) Spaces inside the brackets are required."],["How do you loop over each line of a file safely?","`while read -r line; do echo \"$line\"; done < file.txt`. Unlike `for x in $(cat file)`, this keeps spaces inside each line."],["How do you use a command's output inside a script?","Command substitution: `count=$(grep -c bash /etc/passwd)` or inline `echo \"Today is $(date +%F)\"`. Quote the result when it may contain spaces."]],"4":[["Walk through resetting the root password on RHEL 10.","At GRUB press e, add `rd.break` to the linux line, press Ctrl+X. Then `mount -o remount,rw /sysroot`, `chroot /sysroot`, `passwd root`, `touch /.autorelabel`, exit twice. The relabel fixes the SELinux label on /etc/shadow."],["How do nice values work, and who can change them?","Nice runs from -20 (highest priority) to 19 (lowest), default 0. Start a program with `nice -n 10 cmd`; change a running one with `renice -n 5 -p PID`. Normal users can only raise their own nice value (lower priority); only root can lower it."],["How do you make the journal persistent?","Set `Storage=persistent` in /etc/systemd/journald.conf (or a drop-in in journald.conf.d) or create /var/log/journal, then `systemctl restart systemd-journald`. After that, `journalctl -b -1` shows the previous boot."],["What is the difference between SIGTERM and SIGKILL?","SIGTERM (15, the default for kill) asks the process to exit cleanly and can be caught. SIGKILL (9) is handled by the kernel and cannot be caught or ignored; use it only when SIGTERM fails."],["What does tuned do and how do you choose a profile?","tuned applies sets of kernel and device settings. `tuned-adm recommend` suggests one, `tuned-adm profile virtual-guest` applies it, `tuned-adm active` shows the current one. The tuned service must be enabled."]],"5":[["List the steps to add a new 1 GiB logical volume from a blank disk.","Partition: `parted /dev/vdb mklabel gpt mkpart data 1MiB 100% set 1 lvm on`. Then `pvcreate /dev/vdb1`, `vgcreate vgdata /dev/vdb1`, `lvcreate -L 1G -n lvdata vgdata`, `mkfs.xfs /dev/vgdata/lvdata`, then add it to fstab and mount."],["What is the difference between lvcreate -L and -l?","-L takes a size (500M, 2G). -l takes a number of extents (or a percentage like 100%FREE). With 8 MiB extents, `-l 50` gives 400 MiB. Tasks that ask for extents need -l."],["How do you add persistent swap?","Create a partition (or LV), run `mkswap /dev/vdb2`, get the UUID with blkid, add `UUID=... none swap defaults 0 0` to /etc/fstab, then `swapon -a` and check with `swapon --show` or `free -h`."],["Why mount by UUID instead of /dev/sdX names?","Device names can change between boots when disks are added or found in a different order. A UUID (or label) belongs to the file system itself, so fstab keeps pointing at the right one."]],"6":[["Explain the six fields of an /etc/fstab line.","Device (UUID=, LABEL= or path), mount point, file system type, options (defaults, _netdev, ro), dump flag (usually 0) and fsck order (0 for XFS and network, 1 for root, 2 for others). Test with `mount -a` or `findmnt --verify` before rebooting."],["How do you configure an autofs wildcard map for home directories?","Create /etc/auto.master.d/home.autofs containing `/rhome /etc/auto.rhome`. In /etc/auto.rhome put `* -rw,sync server:/home/&`. Then `systemctl enable --now autofs`. The * matches the key you access and & repeats it."],["How do you extend a logical volume and its file system in one step?","`lvextend -r -L +500M /dev/vg/lv`. The -r option resizes the file system too (xfs_growfs for XFS, resize2fs for ext4). XFS can only grow, never shrink."],["A user gets 'Permission denied' on /data/proj/file even though the file is 644. What do you check?","Each parent directory needs execute (x) for that user: run `namei -l /data/proj/file` to see permissions along the path. Also check ownership, group membership (and log in again after group changes) and SELinux context."]],"7":[["Compare cron and systemd timers for scheduling.","cron uses five time fields in a crontab (`30 2 * * * /path/script`). A systemd timer is a .timer unit paired with a .service, using OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:30:00; it logs to the journal, can catch up missed runs with Persistent=true and is enabled with `systemctl enable --now name.timer`."],["How do you make a system boot into text mode by default?","`systemctl set-default multi-user.target` (check with `systemctl get-default`). To switch now without rebooting, use `systemctl isolate multi-user.target`."],["How do you configure chronyd to use a specific server?","Edit /etc/chrony.conf: replace the pool line with `server time.example.com iburst`. Then `systemctl restart chronyd` (and enable it) and check with `chronyc sources -v`. `timedatectl` shows whether the clock is synchronized."],["How do you add a kernel argument that persists?","`grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args=\"arg=value\"`, or add it to GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX in /etc/default/grub and run `grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg`. Check after reboot with `cat /proc/cmdline`."],["What is the difference between systemctl enable, start and mask?","start runs the unit now. enable creates symlinks so it starts at boot (enable --now does both). mask links the unit to /dev/null so it cannot be started at all, even by hand, until unmasked."]],"8":[["Give the nmcli commands to set a static IPv4 address.","`nmcli con mod eth0 ipv4.addresses 192.168.10.5/24 ipv4.gateway 192.168.10.1 ipv4.dns 192.168.10.1 ipv4.method manual connection.autoconnect yes`, then `nmcli con up eth0`. Check with `ip addr` and `ip route`."],["Where does RHEL 10 store network connection settings?","As NetworkManager keyfiles in /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/*.nmconnection. The old ifcfg-* files in /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts are not used in RHEL 10."],["How do you set the hostname and make it persistent?","`hostnamectl set-hostname server1.example.com` writes /etc/hostname and applies it at once. Add the name to /etc/hosts if it must resolve without DNS."],["What is the difference between a runtime and a permanent firewalld rule?","Without --permanent a rule applies at once but is lost at reload or reboot. With --permanent it is saved but not active until `firewall-cmd --reload`. Common pattern: add with --permanent, then --reload."]],"9":[["How do you create a user with a specific UID, extra group and no login shell?","`useradd -u 2001 -G sysadmins -s /sbin/nologin svc`. Check with `id svc` and `getent passwd svc`."],["How do you force a password change at next login and set aging?","`chage -d 0 alice` forces a change at next login. `chage -M 90 -m 7 -W 14 alice` sets maximum, minimum and warning days. `chage -l alice` shows the settings. New accounts take their defaults from /etc/login.defs."],["What is the danger of `usermod -G` without -a?","-G on its own replaces all of the user's supplementary groups with the list given, so the user may silently lose groups such as wheel. Use `usermod -aG group user` to add."],["How do you give a group passwordless sudo safely?","Create /etc/sudoers.d/admins with `%admins ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL`, then check it with `visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/admins`. The % marks a group; a syntax error in sudoers can lock everyone out of sudo."]],"10":[["Why does a web page in /web return 403 even though permissions are fine?","SELinux: files created under /web get default_t, which httpd_t may not read. Fix with `semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t \"/web(/.*)?\"` then `restorecon -Rv /web`. chcon alone does not survive a relabel."],["How do you let httpd listen on port 8088?","Label the port: `semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8088`. Change Listen in httpd.conf, restart httpd, then `firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8088/tcp` and `firewall-cmd --reload`."],["How do you find out why SELinux blocked something?","Look for AVC messages: `ausearch -m AVC -ts recent`, or grep 'denied' in /var/log/audit/audit.log. With setroubleshoot-server installed, `sealert -a /var/log/audit/audit.log` suggests fixes (a boolean, fcontext or port label)."],["What files and modes do you get with umask 027?","New files: 666 minus 027 = 640 (rw-r-----). New directories: 777 minus 027 = 750 (rwxr-x---). Set it for a user in ~/.bashrc, or system-wide in /etc/login.defs or /etc/profile.d."],["What permissions must ~/.ssh and authorized_keys have for key login?","~/.ssh should be 700 and ~/.ssh/authorized_keys 600, owned by the user, with a correct SELinux context (restorecon -Rv ~/.ssh). Otherwise sshd ignores the keys when StrictModes is on."],["How do you change SELinux mode now and at boot?","`setenforce 0` (permissive) or `setenforce 1` (enforcing) changes it until reboot. For boot, set SELINUX=enforcing or permissive in /etc/selinux/config. Check with `getenforce` or `sestatus`."]]},"qCount":150,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasHandson":true,"hasLevels":true});
